    """Provide an advanced guide to handling numerical precision and stability in GPU-accelerated neural network training and inference. Start by explaining floating-point representation: IEEE 754 standards for FP32, FP16, and BF16, including how precision differs. Discuss sources of numerical instability: loss of significance in subtraction, catastrophic cancellation, and accumulation of rounding errors. Explain how GPU operations (parallel reductions, atomic additions) can introduce different rounding error patterns than sequential CPU computation. Discuss mixed-precision training: using FP16 for computations (faster) but FP32 for accumulation (stability). Explain loss scaling: scaling loss before backpropagation to utilize FP16 range, then unscaling gradients (critical for preventing gradient underflow). Cover gradient accumulation: splitting batches into sub-batches to reduce memory pressure while maintaining stable gradient accumulation. Discuss layer-wise gradient normalization for stability across deep networks. Explain how batch normalization affects numerical stability and how to implement it carefully for different precisions. Cover numerical issues specific to attention mechanisms: computing softmax with large exponents, managing precision in normalization. Discuss validation strategies: comparing GPU results against double-precision CPU baselines, detecting anomalies like NaN propagation. Include techniques for debugging precision issues: tracking value ranges, detecting underflow/overflow. Finally, discuss tradeoffs between speed (lower precision) and stability (higher precision), and practical guidelines for different scenarios.""",

